Tidy data: benefits are stuck in wide format

survey_2017_raw |&gt;
  select(respondent_id, starts_with("benefit_")) |&gt;
  head()







Tip


One respondent has a single true preference, but right now it’s scattered across six separate columns. Before we can do anything useful with it, we need to reshape it with pivot_longer().

Tidy data: pivot_longer() to the rescue

benefits_2017_long &lt;- survey_2017_raw |&gt;
  pivot_longer(
    cols = starts_with("benefit_"),
    names_to = "benefit_type",
    values_to = "response"
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(chosen = response %in% c("Yes", "Y", "yes")) |&gt;
  group_by(respondent_id) |&gt;
  summarize(
    benefit_preference = if (any(chosen)) benefit_type[chosen][1] else NA_character_,
    .groups = "drop"
  )

survey_2017_clean &lt;- survey_2017_raw |&gt;
  select(-starts_with("benefit_")) |&gt;
  left_join(benefits_2017_long, by = "respondent_id")

Strings & expressions: income is a mess

survey_2017_clean |&gt; distinct(income) |&gt; slice_sample(n = 6)

Four different formats mixed together: "85000", "$85,000", "85,000", "85K". Before income can be used in any calculation, it needs to become a real number.

clean_income &lt;- function(x) {
  x &lt;- str_trim(x)
  is_k &lt;- str_detect(x, "K$")
  numeric_part &lt;- x |&gt;
    str_remove_all("[$,K]") |&gt;
    as.numeric()
  if_else(is_k, numeric_part * 1000, numeric_part)
}

survey_2017_clean &lt;- survey_2017_clean |&gt;
  mutate(income = clean_income(income))

survey_2022_clean &lt;- survey_2022_clean |&gt;
  mutate(income = clean_income(income))

A useful side quest: separate()

survey_combined |&gt;
  separate(respondent_id, into = c("survey_year", "respondent_num"), sep = "-") |&gt;
  select(survey_year, respondent_num, everything()) |&gt;
  head()

respondent_id ("2017-0001") is actually two pieces of information squeezed into one column. separate() is pivot_longer()’s cousin for this specific problem — splitting one messy column into several clean ones.

pivot_wider(): the mirror image

survey_combined |&gt;
  count(financial_wellness_cluster, year) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = year, values_from = n)

pivot_longer() took wide data and made it long. pivot_wider() does the opposite — useful here for turning a long count table into a clean side-by-side year comparison, exactly the shape you’d want for a report.

